### 2. $\lim_{x\to0}(\sin^{2}x+\cos^{2}x)$.

1. Por identidad trigonométrica, sabemos que $\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1$.

2. Sustituimos esta identidad en nuestro límite original:
   $$ \lim_{x\to0} (1) $$
3. Dado que el límite de una constante es igual a la misma constante:

$$ \lim_{x\to0}(\sin^{2}x+\cos^{2}x) = 1 $$

```{r}
#| echo: false
#| message: false
#| warning: false
x_vals2 <- seq(-2*pi, 2*pi, length.out = 400)
df_trig <- data.frame(x = round(x_vals2, 2), y = round(sin(x_vals2)^2 + cos(x_vals2)^2, 4))

hchart(df_trig, "line", hcaes(x = x, y = y), color = "#27AE60", name = "f(x)") %>%
  hc_add_series(
    data = data.frame(x = 0, y = 1), type = "scatter", 
    hcaes(x = x, y = y), name = "Evaluación en x=0", color = "#2C3E50",
    marker = list(radius = 6)
  ) %>%
  hc_yAxis(title = list(text = "f(x)"), min = 0, max = 2) %>%
  hc_xAxis(title = list(text = "x"), crosshair = TRUE) %>%
  hc_tooltip(headerFormat = "", pointFormat = "<b>x:</b> {point.x} <br> <b>f(x):</b> {point.y}")
```

